==English==

===Etymology 1===
In imitation of a cry. Used since at least the 18th century.<ref>{{R:OCD2|page=1}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/ɑɹː/|/ɑɹɡ/|lang=en}} <small>growled /ɹ/</small> (♪: <sub>╰—</sub> )
* {{audio|en-ca-argh.ogg|Audio (CA)|lang=en}}

====Interjection====
{{en-interj}}

# {{lb|en|onomatopoeia}} {{non-gloss definition|Expressing [[annoyance]], [[dismay]], [[embarrassment]] or [[frustration]].}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Argh!''' Itʼs already 7:15! Weʼre never gonna make it!}}

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|expression of annoyance}}
* Finnish: {{t+|fi|ääh}}, {{t+|fi|äsh}}, {{t+|fi|äh}}
* French: {{t+|fr|argh|m}}
* German: {{t|de|ohh}}
{{trans-mid}}
* Persian: {{t+|fa|اَه|tr=áhh|sc=fa-Arab}}
* Portuguese: {{t|pt|argh}}
* Russian: {{t|ru|ррр}}, {{t+|ru|ё}}
{{trans-bottom}}

====Usage notes====
Any of the letters may be reduplicated, e.g. ''Arrggh!'', ''Aaaarrrggghhh!!!''

====See also====
* {{l|en|blech}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|argh}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|earg||inert; weak; timid; cowardly}}. Cognate with Scots {{m|sco|ergh}}, {{m|sco|argh}}, {{m|sco|arch}}, {{m|sco|erf||timid; reluctant; unwilling}}. More at {{l|en|eerie}}.

====Adjective====
{{en-adj}}

